WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.279
Here is the FX rate cache from this lesson's lab.

2
00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:11.344
On a miss it fetches the rate, and that fetch is a 100 ms socket read.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:16.453
The method is synchronized, so the read runs while the cache's monitor is held.

4
00:00:16.453 --> 00:00:20.315
In Kotlin, the Synchronized annotation makes the same monitor.

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:25.469
The JDK runs virtual threads on carrier threads, one per core.

6
00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:26.819
Say there are four.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.540
VT 1 to VT 4 call rate for a pair that isn't cached yet.

8
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.080
VT 5 and VT 6 serve requests that never touch the cache.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.388
VT 1 mounts on carrier 1, enters rate, and takes the monitor.

10
00:00:42.388 --> 00:00:47.916
On JDK 21 to 23, the JVM records a monitor's owner per OS thread.

11
00:00:47.916 --> 00:00:50.127
So the owner is carrier 1.

12
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.547
Now VT 1 starts the socket read.

13
00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:56.074
A blocking read normally unmounts a virtual thread.

14
00:00:56.074 --> 00:01:00.293
But while VT 1 holds the monitor, it can't leave its carrier.

15
00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:03.267
It is pinned, and carrier 1 blocks with it.

16
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:06.564
VT 2 mounts on carrier 2 and calls rate.

17
00:01:06.564 --> 00:01:09.274
The monitor is taken, so it blocks.

18
00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:10.591
It is pinned too.

19
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.601
VT 3 and VT 4 do the same.

20
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.961
Now all four carriers are stuck, waiting, while the CPU does nothing.

21
00:01:18.961 --> 00:01:24.208
VT 5 and VT 6 never touch the cache, but no carrier is free to run them.

22
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.615
With four carriers, four callers of rate are enough to stop every virtual thread in the JVM.

23
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.376
Now replace the monitor with a ReentrantLock.

24
00:01:35.376 --> 00:01:39.411
In Kotlin, withLock writes the lock, try and finally for you.

25
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.654
Again, VT 1 to VT 4 mount and call rate.

26
00:01:44.654 --> 00:01:47.029
VT 1 takes the lock first.

27
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.203
VT 1 starts the socket read.

28
00:01:49.203 --> 00:01:53.568
ReentrantLock parks threads the normal way, so VT 1 unmounts.

29
00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:57.503
Its frames go to the heap, and it still holds the lock.

30
00:01:57.733 --> 00:02:00.921
VT 2, VT 3 and VT 4 ask for the lock.

31
00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:03.677
Each one parks and unmounts too.

32
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:05.532
The carriers are free.

33
00:02:05.532 --> 00:02:08.801
Carrier 1 runs VT 5, and carrier 2 runs VT 6.

34
00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:13.596
Nothing sits on a carrier waiting for the network or for the lock.

35
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:20.012
When the rate arrives, VT 1 mounts again on a free carrier, stores the rate and unlocks.

36
00:02:20.012 --> 00:02:21.688
Then VT 2 gets the lock.

37
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:25.634
JDK 24 fixed this case, with JEP 491.

38
00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:34.377
Monitors were reworked so that a virtual thread can take, hold and release them independently of its carrier.

39
00:02:34.377 --> 00:02:39.671
synchronized and Object.wait now unmount, like ReentrantLock does.

40
00:02:39.671 --> 00:02:43.280
JDK 25 is the first LTS release with the fix.

41
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:47.529
So on JDK 25, leave working synchronized code alone.

42
00:02:47.529 --> 00:02:51.670
On JDK 21, put a ReentrantLock around blocking calls.

43
00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:56.436
A few rarer cases still pin, and the next section shows them.
